        detail: "<section class='destination-content'><h2>Overview</h2><p>The Kingdom of Anuradhapura was the first major kingdom and longest-serving capital of ancient Sri Lanka, flourishing from 377 BCE to 1017 CE. Located in the North Central Province, it became the center of Sinhalese civilization, Theravada Buddhism, agriculture, and advanced hydraulic engineering. The ancient city is famous for its enormous stupas, monasteries, royal palaces, sacred sites, and sophisticated irrigation systems that supported a highly developed society for more than 1,300 years. Today, the Ancient City of Anuradhapura is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the most important Buddhist pilgrimage destinations in the world.</p><h2>Historical Background</h2><p>The Kingdom of Anuradhapura was formally established as the capital by King Pandukabhaya around 377 BCE. Under his reign, the city was carefully planned with reservoirs, roads, religious buildings, and administrative structures. Over centuries, many great kings expanded the kingdom, transforming Anuradhapura into a powerful political and cultural center in South Asia.</p><h2>Introduction of Buddhism</h2><p>A major turning point in Anuradhapura's history occurred during the reign of King Devanampiya Tissa (250–210 BCE), when Buddhism was introduced to Sri Lanka by Arahat Mahinda Thera, the son of Emperor Ashoka of India. The arrival of Buddhism transformed the kingdom's culture, architecture, and society. Sanghamitta Theri later brought a sacred sapling of the original Bodhi Tree from India, which was planted as the Sri Maha Bodhi and remains one of the oldest historically documented trees in the world.</p><h2>Golden Age of Anuradhapura</h2><p>The kingdom reached remarkable achievements under rulers such as King Dutugemunu (161–137 BCE), who united Sri Lanka and constructed important monuments including the Ruwanwelisaya Stupa. Later kings built massive stupas, monasteries, hospitals, reservoirs, and irrigation networks, making Anuradhapura one of the most advanced ancient cities in Asia.</p><h2>Ancient Engineering and Irrigation</h2><p>Anuradhapura was famous for its advanced water management systems. Ancient engineers constructed reservoirs, canals, and interconnected irrigation networks that transformed the dry zone into productive agricultural land. Large reservoirs such as Tissa Wewa and Nuwara Wewa demonstrate the engineering knowledge and environmental management skills of ancient Sri Lankan civilization.</p><h2>Why Visit Kingdom of Anuradhapura?</h2><ul><li>Explore a UNESCO World Heritage ancient capital.</li><li>Visit the sacred Sri Maha Bodhi Tree.</li><li>Discover some of the world's largest ancient stupas.</li><li>Experience one of Buddhism's most important pilgrimage destinations.</li><li>Explore ancient monasteries, palaces, and archaeological ruins.</li><li>Learn about Sri Lanka's advanced irrigation civilization.</li></ul><h2>Top Attractions</h2><ul><li>Sri Maha Bodhi Tree</li><li>Ruwanwelisaya Stupa</li><li>Jetavanaramaya Stupa</li><li>Abhayagiri Monastery Complex</li><li>Thuparamaya Stupa</li><li>Lovamahapaya (Brazen Palace)</li><li>Isurumuniya Temple</li><li>Kuttam Pokuna (Twin Ponds)</li><li>Samadhi Buddha Statue</li><li>Tissa Wewa Reservoir</li><li>Mihintale Sacred Mountain</li></ul><h2>Things to Do</h2><ul><li>Explore ancient monasteries and archaeological ruins.</li><li>Visit sacred Buddhist monuments.</li><li>Photograph ancient stupas and stone carvings.</li><li>Cycle through the historic city area.</li><li>Offer worship at Sri Maha Bodhi.</li><li>Learn about ancient Sri Lankan civilization.</li><li>Experience sunrise and sunset around heritage sites.</li></ul><h2>Historical Timeline</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Event</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>377 BCE</td><td>Foundation of Anuradhapura</td><td>King Pandukabhaya established Anuradhapura as the capital of Sri Lanka.</td></tr><tr><td>250 BCE</td><td>Arrival of Buddhism</td><td>Arahat Mahinda Thera introduced Buddhism during King Devanampiya Tissa's reign.</td></tr><tr><td>3rd Century BCE</td><td>Sri Maha Bodhi Arrival</td><td>Sanghamitta Theri brought the sacred Bodhi Tree sapling from India.</td></tr><tr><td>161–137 BCE</td><td>Rule of Dutugemunu</td><td>Major Buddhist monuments including Ruwanwelisaya were constructed.</td></tr><tr><td>1017 CE</td><td>End of Kingdom</td><td>Chola invasions led to the decline and abandonment of Anuradhapura as capital.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Months</th><th>Recommended Activities</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dry Season</td><td>May – September</td><td>Ideal for archaeological exploration and cycling.</td></tr><tr><td>Cool Season</td><td>December – February</td><td>Comfortable sightseeing and pilgrimage visits.</td></tr><tr><td>Festival Season</td><td>May – June</td><td>Religious ceremonies and cultural experiences.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Cultural Significance</h2><p>Anuradhapura remains one of the greatest symbols of Sri Lankan heritage. The city represents the development of Buddhism, ancient governance, architecture, sculpture, literature, and irrigation technology. Its monuments continue to demonstrate the achievements of ancient Sri Lankan civilization and attract pilgrims, historians, and travelers from around the world.</p><h2>Facilities</h2><ul><li>UNESCO archaeological park</li><li>Archaeological museums and visitor centers</li><li>Licensed tourist guides</li><li>Hotels, resorts, and guesthouses</li><li>Restaurants and cafés</li><li>Bicycle rental services</li><li>Parking facilities</li><li>Public restrooms</li><li>Souvenir shops</li><li>Road and railway access</li></ul></section>",
