        detail: "<section class='destination-content'><h2>Overview</h2><p>The Kingdom of Polonnaruwa was the second great capital of ancient Sri Lanka, flourishing from the 11th to the 13th centuries. After the decline of Anuradhapura, Polonnaruwa became the center of political power and reached its golden age under King Parakramabahu I (1153–1186 CE). The kingdom became famous for its advanced irrigation technology, monumental Buddhist architecture, royal complexes, and highly organized urban planning. Today, the Ancient City of Polonnaruwa is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of Sri Lanka's most important archaeological destinations.</p><h2>Historical Background</h2><p>Polonnaruwa became the capital of Sri Lanka after the Chola invasion and the decline of Anuradhapura in the 11th century. King Vijayabahu I (1055–1110 CE) successfully defeated the Chola rulers and established Polonnaruwa as the new center of administration. The kingdom reached its peak during the reign of King Parakramabahu I, who transformed Polonnaruwa into a prosperous medieval city with advanced irrigation systems, religious monuments, and impressive architectural achievements.</p><h2>Golden Age of Polonnaruwa</h2><p>The reign of King Parakramabahu I from 1153 to 1186 CE is considered the golden age of Polonnaruwa. He constructed the massive Parakrama Samudraya reservoir, expanded agriculture, improved trade, and built many temples, palaces, and public structures. His famous statement that not even a drop of rainwater should flow into the ocean without being used for human benefit reflects his commitment to irrigation development.</p><h2>Royal Heritage and Architecture</h2><p>Polonnaruwa contains some of Sri Lanka's finest examples of medieval architecture. The city includes royal palaces, council chambers, image houses, stupas, and beautifully carved stone sculptures. The Gal Vihara, created during the reign of Parakramabahu I, is especially famous for its magnificent Buddha statues carved from a single granite rock.</p><h2>Why Visit Kingdom of Polonnaruwa?</h2><ul><li>Explore a UNESCO World Heritage archaeological city.</li><li>Discover Sri Lanka's medieval royal capital.</li><li>Visit ancient temples, palaces, and stone monuments.</li><li>Experience remarkable irrigation engineering.</li><li>See world-famous Gal Vihara Buddha statues.</li><li>Learn about Sri Lanka's royal history and Buddhist heritage.</li></ul><h2>Top Attractions</h2><ul><li>Royal Palace of King Parakramabahu I</li><li>Gal Vihara</li><li>Parakrama Samudraya Reservoir</li><li>Vatadage</li><li>Rankoth Vehera</li><li>Lankatilaka Image House</li><li>Thuparama Image House</li><li>Nissanka Latha Mandapaya</li><li>Nissankamalla Council Chamber</li><li>Shiva Devale No. 2</li><li>Pabalu Vehera</li></ul><h2>Things to Do</h2><ul><li>Explore ancient ruins by bicycle.</li><li>Visit royal palaces and Buddhist monuments.</li><li>Photograph the Gal Vihara statues.</li><li>Study ancient stone carvings and architecture.</li><li>Enjoy views of Parakrama Samudraya.</li><li>Join guided archaeological tours.</li><li>Learn about medieval Sri Lankan civilization.</li></ul><h2>Historical Timeline</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Event</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1017 CE</td><td>Chola Capture</td><td>The Chola Empire captured Anuradhapura and established control over Sri Lanka.</td></tr><tr><td>1055 CE</td><td>Rise of Vijayabahu I</td><td>King Vijayabahu I defeated the Cholas and restored Sri Lankan independence.</td></tr><tr><td>1153–1186 CE</td><td>Reign of Parakramabahu I</td><td>Polonnaruwa reached its greatest prosperity through irrigation, architecture, and administration.</td></tr><tr><td>1187–1196 CE</td><td>Rule of Nissankamalla</td><td>Further development of monuments and royal structures.</td></tr><tr><td>13th Century</td><td>Decline of Polonnaruwa</td><td>Political instability and invasions led to the abandonment of the city.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Months</th><th>Recommended Activities</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dry Season</td><td>May – September</td><td>Ideal for cycling and exploring archaeological sites.</td></tr><tr><td>Cool Season</td><td>December – February</td><td>Comfortable conditions for heritage tours.</td></tr><tr><td>Tourist Season</td><td>December – April</td><td>Suitable for cultural and historical exploration.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Cultural Significance</h2><p>Polonnaruwa represents one of the greatest achievements of medieval Sri Lankan civilization. The city's temples, reservoirs, sculptures, and urban planning demonstrate the advanced knowledge of engineering, art, religion, and governance during the period. The archaeological site continues to provide valuable insights into Sri Lanka's royal history and Buddhist traditions.</p><h2>Facilities</h2><ul><li>UNESCO archaeological park</li><li>Archaeological museum and visitor center</li><li>Licensed tourist guides</li><li>Bicycle rental services</li><li>Hotels and guesthouses</li><li>Restaurants and cafés</li><li>Parking facilities</li><li>Public restrooms</li><li>Souvenir shops</li><li>Road accessibility</li></ul></section>",
