        detail: "<section class='destination-content'><h2>Overview</h2><p>The Kingdom of Kandy was the last independent Sinhalese kingdom of Sri Lanka, existing from 1469 to 1815. Located in the central highlands, it became the final stronghold of the Sinhalese monarchy after the decline of the Kingdom of Kotte. Surrounded by mountains and dense forests, Kandy successfully resisted Portuguese and Dutch invasions for centuries before coming under British rule through the Kandyan Convention of 1815. Today, the historic city of Kandy is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and remains one of Sri Lanka's most important religious and cultural centers.</p><h2>Historical Background</h2><p>The kingdom was founded in 1469 by Senasammata Vikramabahu, who established an independent kingdom in the hill country. Over the following centuries, Kandyan kings defended the kingdom against repeated invasions by Portuguese and Dutch colonial forces. The difficult mountainous terrain and strong alliances with local chieftains enabled the kingdom to maintain its independence while much of the coastal regions came under European control.</p><h2>Kandyan Resistance Against Colonial Powers</h2><p>The Portuguese launched numerous military campaigns against Kandy between the 16th and early 17th centuries but failed to permanently conquer the kingdom. One of the greatest Kandyan victories occurred during the Battle of Danture in 1594, where Portuguese forces suffered a decisive defeat. Later, the Dutch replaced the Portuguese along the coast but also failed to subdue the kingdom despite several military expeditions. Eventually, internal political conflicts and disputes among Kandyan chiefs weakened the kingdom, allowing the British to annex it peacefully through the Kandyan Convention signed on 2 March 1815.</p><h2>Royal Legacy</h2><p>The Kandyan Kingdom was ruled by both Sinhalese and Nayakkar monarchs. The Nayakkar dynasty, originating from South India, respected and protected Buddhism while continuing many Sinhalese royal traditions. During their reign, temples, palaces, irrigation works, and cultural traditions flourished. The kingdom became the guardian of the Sacred Tooth Relic, the most revered symbol of Buddhist sovereignty in Sri Lanka.</p><h2>Religion and Culture</h2><p>Buddhism remained the foundation of the Kandyan Kingdom. The Temple of the Sacred Tooth Relic (Sri Dalada Maligawa) became the spiritual and political center of the kingdom because possession of the Sacred Tooth Relic symbolized the legitimate right to rule Sri Lanka. Kandyan architecture, traditional dancing, drumming, arts, wood carving, ivory craftsmanship, and temple paintings developed into distinctive cultural traditions that continue today.</p><h2>Why Visit Kingdom of Kandy?</h2><ul><li>Explore the last royal capital of Sri Lanka.</li><li>Visit the Temple of the Sacred Tooth Relic, a UNESCO World Heritage monument.</li><li>Discover royal palaces, museums, and ancient buildings.</li><li>Experience traditional Kandyan dance performances.</li><li>Witness the world-famous Esala Perahera festival.</li><li>Learn about Sri Lanka's resistance against European colonial powers.</li></ul><h2>Top Attractions</h2><ul><li>Temple of the Sacred Tooth Relic (Sri Dalada Maligawa)</li><li>Royal Palace of Kandy</li><li>National Museum of Kandy</li><li>Kandy Lake</li><li>Royal Audience Hall (Magul Maduwa)</li><li>Udawattakele Forest Reserve</li><li>Bahirawakanda Buddha Statue</li><li>Commonwealth War Cemetery</li></ul><h2>Interesting Facts</h2><ul><li>Founded in 1469 by Senasammata Vikramabahu.</li><li>Remained independent for about 346 years.</li><li>The Portuguese never permanently conquered Kandy.</li><li>The Sacred Tooth Relic remains Sri Lanka's most sacred Buddhist relic.</li><li>The city of Kandy became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1988.</li><li>The Esala Perahera is one of Asia's oldest and grandest religious festivals.</li></ul><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Months</th><th>Highlights</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dry Season</td><td>December–April</td><td>Ideal weather for sightseeing and heritage tours.</td></tr><tr><td>Festival Season</td><td>July–August</td><td>Experience the spectacular Esala Perahera procession.</td></tr><tr><td>Rainy Season</td><td>May–November</td><td>Lush landscapes with fewer tourists.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Facilities</h2><ul><li>UNESCO World Heritage attractions</li><li>Museums and archaeological sites</li><li>Licensed tourist guides</li><li>Hotels, guesthouses, and luxury accommodations</li><li>Restaurants and cafés</li><li>Railway station and public transportation</li><li>Parking facilities</li><li>Public restrooms</li><li>Souvenir and handicraft shops</li><li>Banks and ATMs</li></ul></section>",
