        description: "A powerful medieval maritime kingdom of Sri Lanka, centered near Colombo, which reached its golden age under King Parakramabahu VI and became a major center of trade, literature, and culture.",
        detail: "<section class='destination-content'><h2>Overview</h2><p>The Kingdom of Kotte was one of the most influential medieval kingdoms of Sri Lanka, established in the early 15th century near present-day Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte, close to Colombo. The kingdom reached its greatest prosperity under King Parakramabahu VI (1412–1467 CE), who successfully unified most of Sri Lanka under one rule. Kotte became a major political, commercial, and cultural center with strong maritime connections, flourishing literature, and Buddhist traditions.</p><h2>Historical Background</h2><p>The Kingdom of Kotte emerged after the decline of the Gampola Kingdom and the weakening of regional powers in Sri Lanka. The fortified city of Kotte was established by Alagakkonara, a powerful minister during the late Gampola period, and later developed into the capital of an independent kingdom. Its location near important waterways and the western coast allowed Kotte to become an important center for trade and administration.</p><h2>Golden Age of Kotte</h2><p>The kingdom reached its peak during the reign of King Parakramabahu VI (1412–1467 CE). He was the last native ruler to unify the entire island before the arrival of European colonial powers. During his reign, Kotte experienced political stability, economic growth, and cultural development. The period was also known as a golden age of Sinhala literature, with works such as the Sandesha poems being created during this era.</p><h2>Portuguese Arrival and Decline</h2><p>The arrival of the Portuguese in Sri Lanka in 1505 CE changed the political landscape of the island. Kotte rulers gradually became influenced by Portuguese power, and internal conflicts weakened the kingdom. After the death of King Dharmapala, who converted to Christianity and transferred the kingdom to the Portuguese crown, the Kingdom of Kotte officially ended in 1597 CE.</p><h2>Political and Cultural Significance</h2><p>Kotte played an important role in Sri Lanka's medieval history as a center of administration, Buddhism, literature, and international trade. The kingdom contributed significantly to Sinhala poetry, religious scholarship, temple construction, and diplomatic relations with foreign powers.</p><h2>Why Visit Kingdom of Kotte?</h2><ul><li>Discover the history of a powerful medieval Sri Lankan kingdom.</li><li>Explore the ancient capital near modern Colombo.</li><li>Learn about King Parakramabahu VI and Sri Lankan unification.</li><li>Experience the heritage of Sinhala literature and culture.</li><li>Explore historical locations around Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte.</li><li>Understand Sri Lanka's transition into the colonial era.</li></ul><h2>Top Attractions</h2><ul><li>Kotte Raja Maha Viharaya</li><li>Ancient Kotte fortification remains</li><li>Diyatha Uyana waterfront area</li><li>Parliament of Sri Lanka complex</li><li>Welikada historical area</li><li>Nearby temples and cultural sites</li><li>National heritage locations around Kotte</li></ul><h2>Things to Do</h2><ul><li>Explore historical locations around Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte.</li><li>Visit Buddhist temples connected with Kotte heritage.</li><li>Learn about medieval Sri Lankan politics.</li><li>Explore nearby museums and cultural attractions.</li><li>Enjoy walking and sightseeing around the modern capital area.</li><li>Study the literary achievements of the Kotte period.</li></ul><h2>Historical Timeline</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Event</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Early 15th Century</td><td>Foundation of Kotte</td><td>Kotte developed as a fortified capital after the decline of Gampola.</td></tr><tr><td>1412–1467 CE</td><td>Rule of Parakramabahu VI</td><td>The kingdom reached its political and cultural peak and unified Sri Lanka.</td></tr><tr><td>1505 CE</td><td>Portuguese Arrival</td><td>European influence began to transform Sri Lanka's political landscape.</td></tr><tr><td>1597 CE</td><td>End of Kotte Kingdom</td><td>The kingdom was transferred to Portuguese control after King Dharmapala's death.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Months</th><th>Recommended Activities</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dry Season</td><td>January – April</td><td>Comfortable heritage walks and sightseeing.</td></tr><tr><td>Morning Hours</td><td>All year</td><td>Best for exploring outdoor historical locations.</td></tr><tr><td>Weekdays</td><td>Monday – Friday</td><td>Less traffic and peaceful exploration.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Cultural Significance</h2><p>The Kingdom of Kotte represents an important chapter in Sri Lanka's medieval history. Its achievements in literature, diplomacy, trade, and governance influenced the island's cultural development. The kingdom also marked the final period of independent Sri Lankan rule before European colonial expansion.</p><h2>Facilities</h2><ul><li>Historical temples and heritage locations</li><li>Museums and cultural attractions nearby</li><li>Hotels and restaurants in Colombo area</li><li>Public transport availability</li><li>Parking facilities</li><li>Walking areas and parks</li><li>Tourist information services</li></ul></section>",
