        detail: "<section class='destination-content'><h2>Overview</h2><p>The Kingdom of Gampola was one of Sri Lanka's medieval kingdoms, serving as the island's capital from 1341 to 1415 CE. Located in the fertile hill country of present-day Kandy District, Gampola became an important political, religious, and cultural center following the decline of Kurunegala. Surrounded by mountains and rivers, the kingdom was naturally protected from invasions and became known for its royal temples, Buddhist scholarship, and architectural achievements.</p><h2>Historical Background</h2><p>The Kingdom of Gampola was established by King Buwanekabahu IV in 1341 CE after he moved the capital from Kurunegala. The hill-country location offered greater security against South Indian invasions while providing access to fertile agricultural lands. During this period, Gampola strengthened royal authority and continued the traditions of Theravada Buddhism while maintaining diplomatic and commercial relations with neighboring kingdoms.</p><h2>Royal Development</h2><p>Several kings ruled from Gampola, including Buwanekabahu IV, Parakramabahu V, Vikramabahu III, and Buwanekabahu V. One of the kingdom's greatest rulers was King Bhuvanekabahu IV, who initiated the development of the capital and promoted Buddhism. Later rulers continued to support temple construction, irrigation, agriculture, and religious education. The kingdom eventually declined when King Parakramabahu VI shifted the capital to Kotte around 1415 CE.</p><h2>Religious and Cultural Heritage</h2><p>Gampola became an important center of Buddhism and temple architecture. The kingdom is especially associated with Lankatilaka Vihara, Gadaladeniya Temple, and Embekka Devalaya, which remain among Sri Lanka's finest examples of medieval religious architecture. These monuments demonstrate a unique combination of Sinhalese, South Indian, and Dravidian artistic influences and continue to attract pilgrims, historians, and visitors.</p><h2>Architecture and Legacy</h2><p>The temples and monuments constructed during the Gampola period are celebrated for their intricate stone carvings, wooden craftsmanship, decorative pillars, and artistic sculptures. The kingdom played an important role in preserving Buddhism and contributed significantly to the architectural transition between the Polonnaruwa and Kandyan periods.</p><h2>Why Visit Kingdom of Gampola?</h2><ul><li>Explore one of Sri Lanka's medieval hill-country capitals.</li><li>Visit remarkable temples from the Gampola period.</li><li>Discover the history of Sri Lanka's medieval kings.</li><li>Experience outstanding stone and wood carvings.</li><li>Learn about the evolution of Buddhist architecture.</li><li>Enjoy the scenic landscapes of the central highlands.</li></ul><h2>Top Attractions</h2><ul><li>Lankatilaka Vihara</li><li>Gadaladeniya Temple</li><li>Embekka Devalaya</li><li>Ancient Gampola Royal Palace site</li><li>Ambuluwawa Biodiversity Complex</li><li>Mahaweli River surroundings</li></ul><h2>Things to Do</h2><ul><li>Visit medieval Buddhist temples.</li><li>Explore ancient stone and wooden architecture.</li><li>Learn about the Gampola Kingdom's history.</li><li>Photograph heritage monuments.</li><li>Enjoy panoramic hill-country scenery.</li><li>Take guided cultural and archaeological tours.</li></ul><h2>Historical Timeline</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Event</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1341 CE</td><td>Foundation of Gampola</td><td>King Buwanekabahu IV established Gampola as the royal capital.</td></tr><tr><td>14th Century</td><td>Temple Construction</td><td>Lankatilaka, Gadaladeniya, and Embekka became important religious monuments.</td></tr><tr><td>1341–1415 CE</td><td>Medieval Kingdom</td><td>Gampola served as Sri Lanka's political and religious center.</td></tr><tr><td>1415 CE</td><td>Capital Relocated</td><td>King Parakramabahu VI transferred the capital to Kotte.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Months</th><th>Recommended Activities</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dry Season</td><td>January – April</td><td>Ideal weather for temple visits and sightseeing.</td></tr><tr><td>Cool Season</td><td>December – February</td><td>Comfortable conditions for exploring heritage sites.</td></tr><tr><td>Morning</td><td>All Year</td><td>Best lighting for photography and cultural tours.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Cultural Significance</h2><p>The Kingdom of Gampola represents an important transitional period in Sri Lankan history. It strengthened Buddhism, preserved royal traditions, and produced some of the country's finest medieval temples. The artistic achievements of the Gampola period greatly influenced later Kandyan architecture and remain an essential part of Sri Lanka's cultural heritage.</p><h2>Facilities</h2><ul><li>Ancient temples and archaeological sites</li><li>Licensed tourist guides</li><li>Visitor information boards</li><li>Hotels and guesthouses</li><li>Restaurants and cafés</li><li>Parking facilities</li><li>Public restrooms</li><li>Road and railway access</li></ul></section>",
