        detail: "<section class='destination-content'><h2>Overview</h2><p>The Kingdom of Yapahuwa was one of Sri Lanka's medieval capitals, serving as the royal seat from 1272 to 1284 CE. Built around a massive granite rock rising nearly 90 meters above the surrounding plains, Yapahuwa was designed primarily as a fortified citadel to defend the kingdom against South Indian invasions. The kingdom is best known for safeguarding the Sacred Tooth Relic and for its magnificent stone staircase, one of the finest examples of medieval Sri Lankan architecture.</p><h2>Historical Background</h2><p>King Bhuvanekabahu I established Yapahuwa as the capital in 1272 CE after relocating the royal court from Dambadeniya. The naturally defensive rock fortress provided protection during a period of political instability and repeated invasions. The Sacred Tooth Relic and Sacred Bowl Relic, symbols of royal authority, were transferred to Yapahuwa, making it both a political and religious center.</p><h2>Royal Development</h2><p>During the reign of King Bhuvanekabahu I (1272–1284 CE), Yapahuwa flourished as the kingdom's administrative capital. Royal palaces, audience halls, defensive walls, moats, and temples were constructed around the rock fortress. However, after the king's death, South Indian invaders captured the city and carried away the Sacred Tooth Relic, leading to the kingdom's rapid decline and the eventual relocation of the capital to Kurunegala.</p><h2>Architecture and Engineering</h2><p>Yapahuwa is celebrated for its outstanding military architecture. Its iconic stone staircase, richly decorated with intricate carvings and guardian lion sculptures, leads to the summit where the royal palace and sacred structures once stood. The fortress also included defensive walls, gateways, moats, and watchtowers that demonstrated advanced medieval engineering and strategic planning.</p><h2>Religious Importance</h2><p>As the temporary home of the Sacred Tooth Relic, Yapahuwa became an important Buddhist pilgrimage center during the late 13th century. The relic symbolized the legitimacy of Sri Lankan kingship, making the fortress both a religious sanctuary and the political heart of the kingdom.</p><h2>Why Visit Kingdom of Yapahuwa?</h2><ul><li>Climb one of Sri Lanka's most spectacular ancient rock fortresses.</li><li>See the famous stone staircase with intricate carvings.</li><li>Explore the remains of a medieval royal capital.</li><li>Learn about the history of the Sacred Tooth Relic.</li><li>Enjoy panoramic views from the summit.</li><li>Discover impressive military architecture and defensive engineering.</li></ul><h2>Top Attractions</h2><ul><li>Yapahuwa Rock Fortress</li><li>Historic stone staircase</li><li>Lion sculptures and carved entrance</li><li>Royal Palace ruins</li><li>Ancient Buddhist shrine</li><li>Fortification walls and moat</li><li>Yapahuwa Archaeological Museum</li><li>Summit viewpoints</li></ul><h2>Things to Do</h2><ul><li>Climb the historic stone staircase.</li><li>Explore the royal palace ruins.</li><li>Visit the archaeological museum.</li><li>Photograph the fortress and carvings.</li><li>Learn about Sri Lanka's medieval kingdoms.</li><li>Enjoy panoramic views of the surrounding countryside.</li></ul><h2>Historical Timeline</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Event</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1272 CE</td><td>Capital Established</td><td>King Bhuvanekabahu I moved the capital from Dambadeniya to Yapahuwa.</td></tr><tr><td>1272–1284 CE</td><td>Royal Capital</td><td>Yapahuwa served as the political and religious center of the kingdom while housing the Sacred Tooth Relic.</td></tr><tr><td>1284 CE</td><td>South Indian Invasion</td><td>Invaders captured Yapahuwa and carried away the Sacred Tooth Relic.</td></tr><tr><td>Late 13th Century</td><td>Capital Relocated</td><td>The royal capital was later moved to Kurunegala after the fortress fell.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Months</th><th>Recommended Activities</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dry Season</td><td>December – April</td><td>Ideal weather for climbing and exploring the fortress.</td></tr><tr><td>Morning</td><td>All Year</td><td>Cool temperatures and excellent photography conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>Late Afternoon</td><td>All Year</td><td>Beautiful panoramic views and comfortable hiking.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Cultural Significance</h2><p>The Kingdom of Yapahuwa represents one of Sri Lanka's most remarkable examples of medieval military architecture. Although it served as the capital for only a short period, its magnificent fortress, artistic stone carvings, and association with the Sacred Tooth Relic make it one of the country's most important archaeological sites. Today, Yapahuwa stands as a symbol of resilience, craftsmanship, and Sri Lanka's rich medieval heritage.</p><h2>Facilities</h2><ul><li>Archaeological site and protected monument</li><li>Yapahuwa Archaeological Museum</li><li>Licensed tourist guides</li><li>Visitor information boards</li><li>Parking facilities</li><li>Public restrooms</li><li>Hotels and guesthouses nearby</li><li>Restaurants in the surrounding area</li><li>Easy road access from Kurunegala</li></ul></section>",
